# House rules: <Game>

Read these before touching anything. They are what this repository holds itself to.

## 0 · Read first, then build

1. `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`: the canon. Its glossary is binding: Shuttle, not craft; Tick, not step;
   render frame, never "frame" alone. Its laws L1 to L12 outrank anything here.
2. `DECISIONS.md`, then `FICTION.md`, then `PROVENANCE.md`.
3. If you are a worker: your packet in `work/packets/`, and nothing else unless it says so.

Build and test, from the repository's root (PowerShell 7 or Git Bash; absolute paths where UBT wants
them; `<UE>` is the engine's folder):

```
cmake -S core -B build/core -G "Visual Studio 17 2022" -A x64 -DCMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY=MultiThreadedDLL
cmake --build build/core --config Release
ctest --test-dir build/core -C Release --output-on-failure
"<UE>/Engine/Build/BatchFiles/Build.bat" <Game>Editor Win64 Development -Project="<abs>/<Game>.uproject" -WaitMutex
"<UE>/Engine/Binaries/Win64/UnrealEditor-Cmd.exe" "<abs>/<Game>.uproject" -ExecCmds="Automation RunTests <Game>; Quit" -unattended -nullrhi -nosplash -NoSound -log
tools/gates <packet>      # every gate, before any merge
```

All green before a commit. Zero tests run is a failure, not a pass.

## 1 · The Core is the only truth

All world state lives in `core/`: C++20, no engine headers, its own tests, its own CMake. The View
draws a Snapshot and turns the player's hands into Commands. Nothing in Unreal keeps world state: not
an actor, not a component, not a Blueprint, not a static. Nothing in Unreal is read back into the
Core. No actor the Core owns has *Simulate Physics* on, and no engine movement component decides where
a person is. Offline tools may feed data in; nothing in the View feeds state back: precompute in,
telemetry out.

*Enforced by:* the include gate (§3); review (G9); the replay test, which fails if anything outside
the Core changes the outcome.

## 2 · Time is fixed

The Core advances in whole Ticks (<1/120 s for flight; the canon's master Tick is 1/240 s>). The
subsystem adds each render frame's time to a debt, runs whole Ticks, and drops debt past a cap rather
than stretching a Tick. The frame's `DeltaSeconds` never reaches the Core. Nothing that feeds the Core
reads `FPlatformTime`, `FDateTime::Now` or `FMath::Rand`. Global time dilation is not used: the
subsystem's warp is the only control of time. Commands are applied at a Tick index and taped with it.

*Enforced by:* the wall-clock gate over `core/` and the plugin; the frame-rate test (one hash at three
frame rates).

## 3 · No engine in the Core; the C API is the seam

`core/` includes only the C++ standard library. The plugin reaches the Core only through
`core/include/`, a C API, versioned, and frozen for the length of a wave; a change goes in an issue to
the lead, never in a worker's diff. The Core is built by CMake as a static library with precise
floating point (`/fp:precise`, no `/fp:fast`, no `/fp:contract`; `-ffp-contract=off` elsewhere) and the
engine's C runtime (`/MD`), never by UBT with a module's flags.

*Enforced by:* the include gate; the contract hash (G3); a negative control that plants `/fp:fast` and
must fail the determinism gate.

## 4 · Tests with the feature

Write the test with the feature, not after. The Core's tests state physics or a promise, not how the
code is written; they count seconds, never steps. The View's tests are Unreal automation tests, run
headless. A replay is a test: a taped run replays to the same hash on the same build. A visual change
is checked at the named waypoints by screenshots, looked at by someone other than its author; the
screenshots are evidence, kept in `evidence/`, not source.

*Enforced by:* G4, G5, G8.

## 5 · Text is source; binary assets are build outputs

No `.uasset` or `.umap` is made by hand. Meshes come from generators: Blender scripts in
`tools/blender/`, run headless; Geometry Script in C++ in the editor module, baked to Static Meshes
(never run in a packaged game). Materials come from a generator commandlet. MetaSounds are built at
runtime by the Builder API. Input actions and mapping contexts are made in C++. The game mode spawns
the world into an engine map, so there is no level to commit. `Content/Generated/` is not in git and
is rebuilt by `tools/generate` before the editor opens or a cook runs. Every generated asset has a row
in the asset manifest: generator, version, inputs, hash. An exception (a third-party plugin's content,
say) is listed by path in `PROVENANCE.md` with its reason.

**No Blueprint logic.** A Blueprint subclass exists only where the engine insists on an asset, and it
carries no graph.

*Enforced by:* G6 (no binary in git outside the allow-list; regenerating changes nothing).

## 6 · How MCP is used

**Unreal's MCP** (the `ModelContextProtocol` and `AllToolsets` plugins, Experimental in 5.8) is the
agent's eyes and hands in a running editor: look (screenshots, logs, actor lists), try (spawn and
set properties in a scratch level), import generated assets, compile with Live Coding, run the
automation tests. Anything that must exist next time is written as code and committed; an MCP session
is never the only record of how something was made.

- Find tools with `list_toolsets`, `describe_toolset`, `call_tool`; keep the names used in
  `tools/mcp/TOOLSETS.md`.
- Save before a bulk change. Serialise changes that touch the same asset: the server accepts
  concurrent calls and does not keep their order. Read every result; anything not an explicit success
  is a stop.
- One editor per worker, each on its own fixed port, each worktree with its own `.mcp.json`.
- Long jobs (cooking, packaging, lighting) run through UAT or a commandlet from the shell, with the
  editor closed, not through MCP.
- The editor's Python tools (`execute_tool_script`, `execute_python`) are for reading, never for
  making anything that must persist. <Decide: allow them at all?>
- The MCP plugins are editor-only and never in a packaged build. The port never leaves loopback.

**Blender's MCP** (Blender Lab's server, Blender 5.1 or newer) runs committed generators in a
background Blender (`execute_blender_code_for_cli`) and checks what they made (`get_objects_summary`,
`get_object_detail_summary`, `render_thumbnail_to_path`). Every generator also runs without MCP, from
the command line. No cloud model generators.

## 7 · Provenance

No third-party source is vendored without a row in `PROVENANCE.md`: what, which version or commit,
whose, which licence, what is taken. Implement, do not port, from anything whose licence the game
cannot carry: nothing under the GPL, the statically linked LGPL or CC BY-SA, which Unreal's EULA
forbids combining with the engine. Data that ships is open data; nothing from Cesium ion or Google
goes into a build, because neither may be stored offline. The core came from a32x-cbt's
`flight/orbit` at <commit>, under <the licence you chose> (`DECISIONS.md` <D-nnn>).

*Enforced by:* the licence gate over `PROVENANCE.md` and `third_party/`; G9's licence lens.

## 8 · Failure is visible

A load failure, a missing generated asset, a Snapshot block whose length disagrees with the Core's, a
Tape that diverges: each is an error on screen and in the log at startup, never an empty level. Walls
are visible too: anything not built says so, in the HUD, when the player reaches it.

## 9 · The Fiction Budget

`FICTION.md` lists every departure from physics: the drive (2,000 s of specific impulse at
thrust-to-weight 1.2 or more) and the Ring existing in 2038. A second section lists the shortcuts the
slice inherited from the browser sketch (a sphere Earth, no J2, laws that know the exact state, the
Ring placed where the crew arrives, ...), so none is mistaken for a claim. A claim the world makes that
is in neither list and physics does not allow is a bug.

## 10 · Waves, gates and scope

- Work runs in waves. Contracts freeze before a wave starts and change only between waves. Each
  packet has owner directories; a worker touches nothing else, and files an issue instead.
- A worker's "done" is not acceptance: gates G0 to G9 and the lead's review are. Tests and
  implementation of anything with an oracle go to different agents.
- A wave starts on <the owner>'s word and finishes at its gate.
- Never claim fidelity to any real vehicle, place or station.
- Git: commit locally, on a branch; push and pull requests only on <the owner>'s word. Commit messages
  as `<type>: <what>` (`feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `test`), a body that says what changed and
  what it was checked against, and the attribution lines the harness asks for.

## 11 · The shell on Windows

PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`) or Git Bash. Absolute paths for UBT and UAT; forward slashes in paths written
into documents. No heredocs in briefs. C++ for everything that computes; tools in C++, PowerShell or
Node; Python only where the host program requires it (Blender's generators).
